<section vertical-align="center">
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item>
      <h2>How people thinned the shield</h2>
      <p>Some long-lived human-made gases are called <b>ozone-depleting substances</b> because they can start a repeating chain high above Earth.</p>
      <grid recipe="process-steps" spacing="medium">
        <grid-item colspan="15">
          <h4>Stay for years</h4>
          <p>The chemicals remain in the air for a long time.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="15">
          <h4>Drift upward</h4>
          <p>Some eventually reach the stratosphere.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="15">
          <h4>Sunlight opens them</h4>
          <p>UV breaks them apart and releases reactive atoms.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="15">
          <h4>Repeat the damage</h4>
          <p>Those atoms can destroy ozone molecules again and again.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
      <aside variant="info"><p>The protective region became thinner. It was not a neat, visible hole.</p></aside>
      <p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/treaties/montreal-protocol">UNEP, Montreal Protocol</a></p>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>